      "mitigation_strategy": "Adopt a comprehensive risk management approach aligned with EU AI Act Article 9: (1) Identify risks: systematic fairness evaluation highlighted demographic disparities as high‑risk (R‑FAIRNESS‑GAP RPN=80). (2) Implement bias mitigation: collect more representative data, apply fairness‑aware training (e.g., adversarial debiasing), and set per‑group optimal thresholds (note current optimal thresholds differ: young/adult 0.2 vs senior 0.4, suggesting the model is less confident for seniors). (3) Deploy multi‑level monitoring: online tracking of prediction quality by age/gender, with automated fallback to a simpler model if severe drift is detected. (4) Establish a closed‑loop feedback system: clinical users report misclassifications, which are used for periodic fine‑tuning. (5) Document all mitigation measures and validation results to support conformity assessment under the AI Act Annex IV. The goal is to reduce demographic performance gaps and ensure the system maintains acceptable prediction quality across all expected operational demographics.",
      "monitoring_recommendations": "Metric: Selection rate, TPR, FPR and equalized odds gap by age group and gender. Frequency: Daily for selection rate, weekly for TPR/FPR on live data. Acceptance criteria: Disparate impact ratio ≥ 0.8 (per US EEOC guidelines) and equalized odds gap ≤ 0.1.; Metric: Prediction quality drift index (e.g., PSI or distribution of confidence scores) per demographic. Frequency: Weekly. Acceptance criteria: Drift index within ±0.05 of baseline for all groups.; Metric: Model Brier score and ECE by demographic. Frequency: Monthly. Acceptance criteria: No group should have ECE > 0.25 or Brier score > 0.3.; Review frequency: Monthly risk review meeting (including domain experts) to examine fairness dashboards and decide on retraining triggers.",
